WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Reviewer: Anny Chung

00:00:16.212 --> 00:00:18.239
Ok, so I want to start
by asking a question:

00:00:18.239 --> 00:00:20.952
How many of you have had
to fill out some sort of web form

00:00:20.952 --> 00:00:23.960
or even has to read a distorted
sequence of characters like this?

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.087
How many of you found it
really annoying?

00:00:26.087 --> 00:00:27.176
Ok, I understand.

00:00:27.176 --> 00:00:28.417
I invented that.

00:00:28.417 --> 00:00:29.843
(Laughter)

00:00:29.843 --> 00:00:31.769
Well, I was one of the people who did it.

00:00:31.769 --> 00:00:33.282
That thing is called a captcha.

00:00:33.282 --> 00:00:35.613
And the reason it's there
is to make sure that you,

00:00:35.613 --> 00:00:37.962
the entity filling out the form,
are actually a human,

00:00:37.962 --> 00:00:39.436
and not a computer program

00:00:39.436 --> 00:00:41.445
written to submit
the form millions of times.

00:00:41.445 --> 00:00:43.274
The reason it works 
is because humans,

00:00:43.274 --> 00:00:45.143
at least, non-visually-impaired humans,

00:00:45.143 --> 00:00:47.333
have no trouble reading
these distorted characters,

00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:49.911
whereas computer programs
simply can't do it as well yet.

00:00:49.911 --> 00:00:51.776
For example,
in the case of Ticket Master

00:00:51.776 --> 00:00:54.188
the reason you have to type
these distorted characters

00:00:54.188 --> 00:00:56.543
is to prevent scalpers to write
a program that can buy

00:00:56.543 --> 00:00:58.119
millions of ticket at a time.

00:00:58.119 --> 00:00:59.918
Captchas are used all over the Internet

00:00:59.918 --> 00:01:01.951
and they are used so often
that a lot of times

00:01:01.951 --> 00:01:05.030
the precise sequence of random characters
that are shown to the user

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:06.025
is not so fortunate.

00:01:06.025 --> 00:01:08.535
This is an example from
the Yahoo registration page.

00:01:08.535 --> 00:01:09.301
[W A I T]

00:01:09.301 --> 00:01:12.012
Random characters
that happen to be shown to the user

00:01:12.012 --> 00:01:14.118
were W, A, I, T, 
which of course spell a word.

00:01:14.118 --> 00:01:16.434
But the best part is the message

00:01:16.434 --> 00:01:18.661
that the Yahoo Help got
about 20 minutes later.

00:01:18.661 --> 00:01:24.661
["Help! I've been waiting 
for 20 minutes, and nothing happens!"]

00:01:24.661 --> 00:01:27.384
(Laughter)

00:01:27.384 --> 00:01:30.390
This of course is not as bad
as this poor person who...

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.095
[R E S T A R T]

00:01:32.095 --> 00:01:33.533
(Laughter)

00:01:33.533 --> 00:01:36.048
Ok, now, this Captcha project
was something that we did

00:01:36.048 --> 00:01:37.201
here at Carnegie Mellon

00:01:37.201 --> 00:01:39.305
about ten years ago
and is used everywhere.

00:01:39.305 --> 00:01:42.244
Let me now tell you about a project
that we did a few years later,

00:01:42.244 --> 00:01:44.506
which is sort of the next evolution
of captchas.

00:01:44.506 --> 00:01:46.540
This is the project
that we called Recaptcha,

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.200
which is something that we started
here at Carnegie Melon,

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.086
then we turned it into a startup company,

00:01:51.086 --> 00:01:52.775
and then about a year and a half ago

00:01:52.775 --> 00:01:54.521
Google actually acquired this company.

00:01:54.521 --> 00:01:57.166
So, let me tell you
[how] this project started.

00:01:57.166 --> 00:01:59.907
So this project started
from the following realization:

00:01:59.907 --> 00:02:02.533
it turns out that approximately 
200 million captchas

00:02:02.533 --> 00:02:04.771
are typed every day
by people around the world.

00:02:04.771 --> 00:02:07.186
When I first heard this
I was quite proud of myself

00:02:07.186 --> 00:02:10.036
I thought, "Look at the impact
that my research has had."

00:02:10.098 --> 00:02:11.622
But then I started feeling bad,

00:02:11.622 --> 00:02:13.939
see here is the thing:
each time you type a captcha,

00:02:13.939 --> 00:02:16.208
essentially, you waste
ten seconds of your time.

00:02:16.208 --> 00:02:18.751
Because it takes
ten seconds to type a captcha --

00:02:18.751 --> 00:02:22.057
and if you multiply that by 200 million
you get that humanity as a whole

00:02:22.057 --> 00:02:24.164
is wasting about 500
thousand hours every day

00:02:24.164 --> 00:02:26.137
typing these annoying captchas.
(Laughter)

00:02:26.137 --> 00:02:27.540
So then I started feeling bad,

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:28.964
(Laughter)

00:02:28.964 --> 00:02:31.423
and I started thinking,
"Well, of course we can't just

00:02:31.423 --> 00:02:33.744
get rid of captchas,
because the security of the Web

00:02:33.744 --> 00:02:36.298
sort of depends on them,
but then I started thinking,

00:02:36.298 --> 00:02:38.266
"Is there any way we can use this effort

00:02:38.266 --> 00:02:40.389
for something that is good for humanity?"

00:02:40.389 --> 00:02:42.559
Here is the thing,
while you're typing a captcha,

00:02:42.559 --> 00:02:43.784
during those ten seconds,

00:02:43.784 --> 00:02:45.543
your brain is doing something amazing.

00:02:45.543 --> 00:02:48.093
It is doing something
that computers cannot yet do.

00:02:48.093 --> 00:02:51.005
So, can we get you to do useful work
for those ten seconds?

00:02:51.007 --> 00:02:52.507
Another way of putting it is:

00:02:52.507 --> 00:02:56.004
Is there some humongous problem
that we cannot get yet computers to solve,

00:02:56.004 --> 00:02:58.421
that somehow we can split
into tiny ten second chunks

00:02:58.421 --> 00:03:00.516
such that each time
somebody solves a captcha

00:03:00.516 --> 00:03:02.358
they solve a little bit of this problem?

00:03:02.358 --> 00:03:04.796
The answer is yes,
and this is what we are doing now.

00:03:04.796 --> 00:03:06.529
What you may not know
is that nowadays,

00:03:06.529 --> 00:03:07.882
while you're typing a captcha

00:03:07.882 --> 00:03:10.250
not only are you
authenticating yourself as a human

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:13.056
but in addition you are actually
helping us to digitize books.

00:03:13.056 --> 00:03:14.393
Let me explain how it works.

00:03:14.399 --> 00:03:16.604
A lot of projects
are trying to digitize books.

00:03:16.604 --> 00:03:18.795
Google has one,
the Internet Archive has one,

00:03:18.795 --> 00:03:21.165
Amazon, with the Kindle,
is trying to digitize books.

00:03:21.165 --> 00:03:23.817
The way is works is, you start
with an old book, a physical thing,

00:03:23.817 --> 00:03:26.193
you've seen those things right? 
Like a... book...

00:03:26.193 --> 00:03:27.772
(Laughter)

00:03:27.772 --> 00:03:30.482
So, you start with a book,
and then you scan it.

00:03:30.482 --> 00:03:32.742
Scanning a book is like
taking a digital photograph

00:03:32.742 --> 00:03:34.181
of every page of the book.

00:03:34.181 --> 00:03:36.191
It gives you an image
for every page of the book.

00:03:36.191 --> 00:03:38.522
This is an image with text
for every page of the book.

00:03:38.522 --> 00:03:41.221
The next step in the process
is that the computer needs to be able

00:03:41.221 --> 00:03:43.256
to decipher all of the words
in this image.

00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:45.310
That is done using a tecnology called OCR,

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:46.548
Optical Character Recognition,

00:03:46.548 --> 00:03:48.053
which takes a picture of text

00:03:48.053 --> 00:03:50.203
and tries to figure out
what text is in there.

00:03:50.203 --> 00:03:53.114
Now, the problem is
that OCR is not perfect,

00:03:53.114 --> 00:03:55.450
especially for older books,
where the ink has faded,

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:57.279
and the pages have turned yellow,

00:03:57.279 --> 00:03:59.106
OCR cannot recognize a lot of the words.

00:03:59.106 --> 00:04:01.671
For things that were written
more than fifty years ago,

00:04:01.671 --> 00:04:04.976
the computer cannot recognize about
thirty percent of the words.

00:04:05.079 --> 00:04:08.398
We are now taking all of the words
that the computer cannot recognize

00:04:08.398 --> 00:04:10.530
and we are getting people
to read them for us,

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.853
while they are typing
a captcha on the Internet.

00:04:12.853 --> 00:04:14.334
So next time you type a captcha,

00:04:14.334 --> 00:04:16.727
these words that you're typing
are actually words

00:04:16.727 --> 00:04:19.131
that are coming from
books that are being digitized

00:04:19.131 --> 00:04:20.789
that the computer could not recognize.

00:04:20.789 --> 00:04:23.236
The reason we have two words nowadays
instead of one,

00:04:23.236 --> 00:04:25.080
is because one of the words

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:27.339
is a word that the system
just got out of a book,

00:04:27.339 --> 00:04:28.529
it didn't know what it was

00:04:28.529 --> 00:04:30.263
and it's going to present it to you,

00:04:30.263 --> 00:04:32.238
but since it doesn't know
the answer for it,

00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:33.479
it cannot grade it for you,

00:04:33.479 --> 00:04:34.979
so we give you another word,

00:04:34.979 --> 00:04:37.099
one for which the system
does know the answer,

00:04:37.099 --> 00:04:40.032
we don't tell you which one is which,
and we say, please type both.

00:04:40.032 --> 00:04:41.546
If you type the correct word

00:04:41.546 --> 00:04:43.621
for the one which the system
already knows,

00:04:43.621 --> 00:04:45.011
it assumes you are a human,

00:04:45.011 --> 00:04:48.229
and it also gets some confidence
that you typed the other word correctly.

00:04:48.229 --> 00:04:50.630
And if we repeat this process
to ten different people,

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:52.768
and all of them
agree on what the new word is,

00:04:52.768 --> 00:04:54.853
then we have one more word
digitized accurately.

00:04:54.853 --> 00:04:56.239
This is how the system works,

00:04:56.239 --> 00:04:58.668
and since we released it
about 3 or 4 years ago,

00:04:58.668 --> 00:05:00.673
a lot of websites started switching from

00:05:00.673 --> 00:05:02.809
the old captcha,
where people wasted their time,

00:05:02.809 --> 00:05:06.014
to the new captcha, where people
are helping to digitize books.

00:05:06.114 --> 00:05:08.346
So every time you
buy tickets on Ticket Master,

00:05:08.346 --> 00:05:09.786
you help to digitize a book.

00:05:09.786 --> 00:05:11.512
Facebook, every time you add a friend,

00:05:11.512 --> 00:05:13.487
or poke somebody,
you help digitize a book.

00:05:13.487 --> 00:05:15.544
Twitter, and about
350 thousand other sites

00:05:15.544 --> 00:05:17.778
are all using recaptcha.
And in fact, the number of sites

00:05:17.778 --> 00:05:19.365
that are using recaptcha is so high

00:05:19.365 --> 00:05:21.652
that the number of words
that we're digitizing per day

00:05:21.652 --> 00:05:23.680
is really large.
It's about 100 million a day

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:25.711
which is the equivalent of about

00:05:25.711 --> 00:05:27.537
2.5 million books a year.

00:05:27.537 --> 00:05:29.696
And this is all being done
one word at a time

00:05:29.696 --> 00:05:31.952
by just people typing captchas
on the Internet.

00:05:31.952 --> 00:05:36.961
(Applause)

00:05:40.299 --> 00:05:43.421
Of course, since we are doing
so many words per day

00:05:43.421 --> 00:05:46.112
funny things can happen,
and this is especially true

00:05:46.112 --> 00:05:47.834
because now we're giving people 

00:05:47.834 --> 00:05:50.391
two randomly-chosen
English words next to each other.

00:05:50.391 --> 00:05:52.305
So funny things can happen,
for example,

00:05:52.305 --> 00:05:54.828
we presented this word,
it's the word "christians",

00:05:54.828 --> 00:05:56.395
there's nothing wrong with it,

00:05:56.395 --> 00:05:59.152
but if you present it along
with another randomly chosen word,

00:05:59.152 --> 00:06:00.346
bad things can happen:

00:06:00.346 --> 00:06:02.494
["bad christians"]
(Laughter)

00:06:02.494 --> 00:06:04.633
But it's even worse
because the particular website

00:06:04.633 --> 00:06:07.386
where we showed this
actually happened to be called

00:06:07.386 --> 00:06:09.291
The Embassy of the Kingdom of God.

00:06:09.291 --> 00:06:12.783
(Laughter)

00:06:16.167 --> 00:06:18.207
Here is another really bad one:

00:06:18.207 --> 00:06:19.889
johnedwards.com

00:06:19.889 --> 00:06:22.203
["damn liberal"]

00:06:22.203 --> 00:06:25.140
(Laughter)

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:27.772
So we keep on insulting people
left and right every day.

00:06:27.772 --> 00:06:29.625
Of course we are not just
insulting people,

00:06:29.625 --> 00:06:31.570
see, here is the thing,
since we're presenting

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:33.015
two randomly chosen words,

00:06:33.015 --> 00:06:34.361
just interesting things can happen.

00:06:34.361 --> 00:06:36.815
So this actually has given rise to

00:06:36.815 --> 00:06:39.663
a really big Internet meme

00:06:39.663 --> 00:06:42.264
that tens of thousands of people
have participated in,

00:06:42.264 --> 00:06:43.491
which is called Captcha Art.

00:06:43.491 --> 00:06:45.550
I'm sure some of you have heard about it,

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:48.398
here's how it works:
imagine you're using the Internet

00:06:48.398 --> 00:06:50.051
and you see a captcha that you think

00:06:50.051 --> 00:06:51.294
is somewhat peculiar.

00:06:51.294 --> 00:06:53.537
Like this captcha: 
["invisible toaster"]

00:06:53.537 --> 00:06:55.819
Then you take a screenshot of it,

00:06:55.819 --> 00:06:57.726
-- of course you fill out the captcha

00:06:57.726 --> 00:06:59.473
because it helps us digitize a book --

00:06:59.473 --> 00:07:01.237
but then, first you take a screenshot

00:07:01.237 --> 00:07:04.043
and then you draw something 
that is related to it.

00:07:04.043 --> 00:07:05.796
(Laughter)

00:07:05.796 --> 00:07:08.427
That's how it works.

00:07:08.427 --> 00:07:11.044
There are tens of thousands of these,

00:07:11.044 --> 00:07:14.651
some of them are very cute,

00:07:14.651 --> 00:07:16.895
[clenched it]

00:07:16.895 --> 00:07:18.971
some of them are funnier,

00:07:18.981 --> 00:07:21.099
[stoned founders]

00:07:21.099 --> 00:07:24.133
(Laughter)

00:07:24.133 --> 00:07:29.443
and some of them, 
like "paleontological shvisle",

00:07:29.443 --> 00:07:30.957
they contain Snoop Dog.

00:07:30.957 --> 00:07:32.609
(Laughter)

00:07:33.917 --> 00:07:36.338
Ok, so this is my
favorite number of recaptchas

00:07:36.338 --> 00:07:39.218
this is the favorite thing that I like
about this whole project.

00:07:39.218 --> 00:07:41.162
This is the number
of distinct people

00:07:41.162 --> 00:07:43.907
that have helped us digitize
at least one word out of a book

00:07:43.907 --> 00:07:46.197
through recaptcha:
750 million, which is a little over

00:07:46.197 --> 00:07:48.283
than ten percent of the world's population

00:07:48.283 --> 00:07:50.276
has helped us digitize human knowledge.

00:07:50.276 --> 00:07:53.690
And it is numbers like these that motivate
my research agenda.

00:07:53.690 --> 00:07:56.508
The question that motivates
my research is the following:

00:07:56.508 --> 00:07:58.921
if you look at humanity's
large scale achievements

00:07:58.921 --> 00:08:00.671
these really big things that humanity

00:08:00.671 --> 00:08:02.812
has gotten together and done historically,

00:08:02.812 --> 00:08:05.335
like for example,
building the pyramids of Egypt,

00:08:05.335 --> 00:08:08.684
or the Panama canal,
or putting a man on the moon,

00:08:08.684 --> 00:08:10.580
there's a curious fact about them.

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:12.100
And it is that they were all done

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:13.944
with about the same number of people.

00:08:13.944 --> 00:08:16.677
It's weird. They were all done with about
100 thousand people

00:08:16.677 --> 00:08:18.284
And the reason for that is because

00:08:18.284 --> 00:08:20.392
before the Internet, coordinating

00:08:20.392 --> 00:08:22.049
more than 100 thousand people

00:08:22.049 --> 00:08:24.562
let alone paying them,
was essentially impossible.

00:08:24.562 --> 00:08:26.176
But see now with the Internet,

00:08:26.176 --> 00:08:28.265
I've just shown you a project
where we've gotten

00:08:28.265 --> 00:08:30.417
750 million people
to help us digitize human knowledge.

00:08:30.417 --> 00:08:32.413
So the question that
motivates my research is

00:08:32.413 --> 00:08:34.742
if we can put a man on the moon
with 100 thousand

00:08:34.742 --> 00:08:37.229
what can we do with 100 million?

00:08:37.229 --> 00:08:39.261
So based on this question
we've had a lot

00:08:39.261 --> 00:08:41.384
of different projects
that we've been working on,

00:08:41.384 --> 00:08:43.589
let me tell you about one
that I'm most excited about

00:08:43.589 --> 00:08:46.451
this is something that we've been
sort of semi-quietly working on

00:08:46.451 --> 00:08:48.070
for the last year and a half or so.

00:08:48.070 --> 00:08:50.697
It hasn't yet been launched,
it's called Duolingo,

00:08:50.697 --> 00:08:53.285
since it hasn't been launched, shhh!

00:08:53.285 --> 00:08:54.677
(Laughter)

00:08:54.677 --> 00:08:56.554
Ah, yeah, I can trust you....

00:08:56.554 --> 00:08:58.493
Ok, so here is how it started:

00:08:58.493 --> 00:09:01.408
it started with me posing a question
to my graduate student,

00:09:01.408 --> 00:09:04.752
Severin Hacker
-- this clicker is not working so well --

00:09:04.752 --> 00:09:06.159
ok that's Severin Hacker,

00:09:06.159 --> 00:09:08.916
so I posed a question 
to my graduate student

00:09:08.916 --> 00:09:11.047
by the way, you did hear me correctly,

00:09:11.047 --> 00:09:12.981
his last name is Hacker,

00:09:12.981 --> 00:09:14.856
so I posed this question to him:

00:09:14.856 --> 00:09:17.783
how can we get 100 million people

00:09:17.783 --> 00:09:20.633
translating the Web
into every major language for free?

00:09:20.633 --> 00:09:22.908
There's a lot of things to say
about this question.

00:09:22.908 --> 00:09:24.663
First of all, translating the Web.

00:09:24.663 --> 00:09:27.390
So, right now the Web is partitioned
into multiple languages,

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:29.234
a large fraction of it is in English,

00:09:29.234 --> 00:09:31.406
if you don't know any English
you can't access it,

00:09:31.406 --> 00:09:33.953
but there's large fractions
in other different languages,

00:09:33.953 --> 00:09:36.615
and if you don't know those languages
you can't access it.

00:09:36.615 --> 00:09:38.507
So I would like to translate
all of the web

00:09:38.507 --> 00:09:40.977
or at least, most of it
into every major language.

00:09:40.977 --> 00:09:43.035
So that's what I would like to do.

00:09:43.035 --> 00:09:44.488
Now, some of you may say,

00:09:44.488 --> 00:09:46.407
why can't we use
computers to translate,

00:09:46.407 --> 00:09:48.031
why can't we use machine translation,

00:09:48.031 --> 00:09:51.101
which nowadays is starting to translate
some sentences here and there,

00:09:51.101 --> 00:09:53.323
why can't we use it
to translate the whole Web?

00:09:53.323 --> 00:09:55.496
Well, the problem is,
it's not yet good enough,

00:09:55.496 --> 00:09:57.843
and it probably won't be
for the next 15 to 20 years.

00:09:57.843 --> 00:10:00.256
It makes a lot of mistakes,
and even when it doesn't

00:10:00.256 --> 00:10:03.605
since it makes so many mistakes
you don't know whether to trust it or not.

00:10:03.605 --> 00:10:05.224
Let me show you an example

00:10:05.224 --> 00:10:07.511
of something that was
translated with a machine,

00:10:07.511 --> 00:10:09.699
it was actually a forum post with someone

00:10:09.699 --> 00:10:12.523
who was trying to ask
a question about Javascript.

00:10:12.523 --> 00:10:15.268
It was translated
from Japanese into English.

00:10:15.268 --> 00:10:16.869
Let's see if this works.

00:10:16.869 --> 00:10:19.419
I'll just let you read,
this person starts apologizing

00:10:19.419 --> 00:10:21.852
for the fact that it's translated
with the computer.

00:10:21.852 --> 00:10:25.235
[This is question, English is faulty.
Thank computer to translate. Sorry!]

00:10:25.235 --> 00:10:27.768
The next sentence is going to be
the preamble to the question

00:10:27.768 --> 00:10:29.484
he's just explaining something,

00:10:29.484 --> 00:10:31.500
remember, it's a question
about Javascript:

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:34.946
[At often, the goat-time 
install a error is vomit.]

00:10:34.946 --> 00:10:37.774
(Laughter)

00:10:38.605 --> 00:10:42.453
Then comes the first part of the question:

00:10:42.453 --> 00:10:47.908
[How many times like the wind,
a pole, and the dragon?]

00:10:47.908 --> 00:10:49.824
(Laughter)

00:10:49.824 --> 00:10:52.339
Then comes my favorite
part of the question:

00:10:52.339 --> 00:10:56.405
[This insult to father's stones?]

00:10:56.405 --> 00:10:58.483
(Laughter)

00:10:58.483 --> 00:10:59.952
And then comes the ending,

00:10:59.952 --> 00:11:02.117
which is my favorite part
of the whole thing:

00:11:02.117 --> 00:11:05.536
[Please apologize for your stupidity.
There are a many thank you.]

00:11:05.536 --> 00:11:07.302
(Laughter)

00:11:07.302 --> 00:11:10.244
Ok, so computer translation,
not yet good enough.

00:11:10.244 --> 00:11:14.611
Ok? So back to the question,
we need people to translate the whole Web.

00:11:15.175 --> 00:11:17.134
Now the next question you may have is

00:11:17.134 --> 00:11:19.288
"Why can't we just
pay people to do this,

00:11:19.288 --> 00:11:21.576
we could pay professional
language translators

00:11:21.576 --> 00:11:23.018
to translate the whole Web".

00:11:23.018 --> 00:11:24.475
We could do that.

00:11:24.475 --> 00:11:26.372
Unfortunately,
it'd be extremely expensive.

00:11:26.372 --> 00:11:28.467
For example,
translating a tiny tiny fraction

00:11:28.467 --> 00:11:29.915
of the whole Web, Wikipedia,

00:11:29.915 --> 00:11:32.255
into one other language, Spanish.

00:11:32.255 --> 00:11:34.290
You know, Wikipedia exists in Spanish,

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:36.799
but it is very small
compared to the size of English,

00:11:36.799 --> 00:11:38.569
it's about 20% the size of English.

00:11:38.569 --> 00:11:40.942
If you want to translate
the other 80% into Spanish

00:11:40.942 --> 00:11:43.136
it would cost at least 50 million dollars.

00:11:43.136 --> 00:11:46.263
This is even at the most exploited
outsourcing country out there.

00:11:46.263 --> 00:11:47.464
(Laughter)

00:11:47.464 --> 00:11:49.539
So, it'd very expensive.
What we want to do

00:11:49.539 --> 00:11:51.766
is to get 100 million people
translating the Web

00:11:51.766 --> 00:11:53.747
into every major language for free.

00:11:53.747 --> 00:11:55.598
Now, if this is what you want to do

00:11:55.598 --> 00:11:57.937
you pretty quickly realize
you're going to run into

00:11:57.937 --> 00:12:00.091
two pretty big hurdles, two big obstacles.

00:12:00.091 --> 00:12:03.418
The first one is a lack of bilinguals.

00:12:04.233 --> 00:12:07.134
I don't even know if there exists
100 million people out there

00:12:07.134 --> 00:12:10.321
using the Web who are bilingual enough
to help us translate.

00:12:10.321 --> 00:12:11.698
That's a big problem.

00:12:11.698 --> 00:12:13.765
The other problem
that you're going to run into

00:12:13.765 --> 00:12:15.233
is the lack of motivation.

00:12:15.233 --> 00:12:16.906
How are we going to motivate people

00:12:16.906 --> 00:12:18.836
to actually translate the Web for free?

00:12:18.836 --> 00:12:20.961
Normally, you have to pay people
to do this,

00:12:20.961 --> 00:12:23.734
so how are you going to motivate them
to do it for free?

00:12:23.734 --> 00:12:25.971
Now, when we were
starting to think about this

00:12:25.971 --> 00:12:27.487
we were blocked by these two things

00:12:27.487 --> 00:12:29.553
but then we realized
that there's actually a way

00:12:29.553 --> 00:12:31.790
to solve both these problems 
with the same solution.

00:12:31.790 --> 00:12:33.773
There's a way to kill two birds 
with one stone.

00:12:33.773 --> 00:12:35.849
And that is to transform
language translation into

00:12:35.849 --> 00:12:38.015
something that millions
of people want to do

00:12:38.015 --> 00:12:40.249
and that also helps with the problem

00:12:40.249 --> 00:12:41.597
of a lack of bilinguals.

00:12:41.597 --> 00:12:43.588
And that is language education.

00:12:43.588 --> 00:12:47.665
So, it turns out that, today,
there are over 1.2 billion people

00:12:47.665 --> 00:12:49.553
learning a foreign language.

00:12:49.553 --> 00:12:51.726
People really want to learn
a foreign language,

00:12:51.726 --> 00:12:54.163
and it's not just because they're being
forced to do so in school.

00:12:54.163 --> 00:12:56.093
For example, in the United States alone

00:12:56.093 --> 00:12:58.458
there are 5 million people
who have paid over 500 dollars

00:12:58.458 --> 00:13:00.435
for software to learn a new language.

00:13:00.435 --> 00:13:02.489
So people really want
to learn a new language.

00:13:02.489 --> 00:13:04.889
What we've been working on
for the last year and half

00:13:04.889 --> 00:13:06.054
is a new website.

00:13:06.054 --> 00:13:07.219
It's called Duolingo.

00:13:07.219 --> 00:13:08.855
Where the basic idea is

00:13:08.855 --> 00:13:13.202
as people use Duolingo,
people learn a new language for free

00:13:13.202 --> 00:13:17.096
while simultaneously translating the Web.

00:13:17.096 --> 00:13:20.480
Basically they are learning by doing.

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:21.739
The way this works is

00:13:21.739 --> 00:13:24.838
whenever you're just a beginner
we give you very simple sentences,

00:13:24.838 --> 00:13:27.702
there's of course a lot of
very simple sentences on the Web.

00:13:27.702 --> 00:13:29.276
We give you very simple sentences

00:13:29.276 --> 00:13:30.785
along with what each word means.

00:13:30.785 --> 00:13:32.423
And as you translate them,

00:13:32.423 --> 00:13:34.549
and as you see how
other people translate them,

00:13:34.549 --> 00:13:37.155
you start learning the language,
as you get more advanced,

00:13:37.155 --> 00:13:39.318
we give you more
complex sentences to translate.

00:13:39.318 --> 00:13:41.347
But at all times,
you are learning by doing.

00:13:41.347 --> 00:13:42.890
The crazy thing about this method

00:13:42.890 --> 00:13:45.254
is that it actually really works.

00:13:45.254 --> 00:13:47.591
First of all, people are
really learning a language

00:13:47.591 --> 00:13:49.871
we're mostly done building,
and we are now testing it

00:13:49.871 --> 00:13:52.002
people really can learn
a language with it.

00:13:52.002 --> 00:13:55.295
And they learn it about as well as
the leading language learning software.

00:13:55.295 --> 00:13:56.682
So people really do learn a language.

00:13:56.682 --> 00:13:59.914
And not only do they learn it as well,
but actually it's way more interesting.

00:13:59.914 --> 00:14:01.287
Because, you see, with Duolingo

00:14:01.287 --> 00:14:03.320
people are actually learning
with real content.

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:05.018
As opposed to learning
with made-up sentences

00:14:05.018 --> 00:14:07.007
people are learning with real content,

00:14:07.007 --> 00:14:08.536
which is inherently interesting.

00:14:08.536 --> 00:14:10.397
So people really do learn a language,

00:14:10.397 --> 00:14:12.612
but perhaps, more surprisingly,

00:14:12.612 --> 00:14:14.738
the translations that we get from people

00:14:14.738 --> 00:14:17.329
using the site,
even though they're just beginners,

00:14:17.329 --> 00:14:19.700
the translations that we get
are as accurate

00:14:19.700 --> 00:14:21.755
as those of professional
language translators,

00:14:21.755 --> 00:14:23.147
which is very surprising.

00:14:23.147 --> 00:14:24.453
Let me show you one example,
this is a sentence

00:14:24.453 --> 00:14:26.438
that was translated
from German into English

00:14:26.438 --> 00:14:28.528
the top is the German,

00:14:28.528 --> 00:14:30.421
the middle is an English translation

00:14:30.421 --> 00:14:33.396
that was done by somebody who was
a professional language translator,

00:14:33.396 --> 00:14:35.770
who we've paid 20 cents a word
for this translation,

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:37.760
and the bottom is a translation
by users of Duolingo,

00:14:37.760 --> 00:14:39.963
none of whom knew any German

00:14:39.963 --> 00:14:41.537
before they started using the site.

00:14:41.537 --> 00:14:43.940
As you can see, it's pretty much perfect.

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:45.654
Now, of course we played a trick here

00:14:45.654 --> 00:14:47.355
to make the translations as good as

00:14:47.355 --> 00:14:49.252
professional language translators.

00:14:49.252 --> 00:14:51.344
We combined the translations
of multiple beginners

00:14:51.344 --> 00:14:54.177
to get the quality
of a single professional translator.

00:14:54.177 --> 00:14:58.264
Even though we're
combining the translations

00:14:58.264 --> 00:15:01.703
the site actually
can translate pretty fast.

00:15:01.703 --> 00:15:03.510
Let me show you, these are estimates

00:15:03.510 --> 00:15:05.786
of how fast we could translate Wikipedia

00:15:05.786 --> 00:15:07.387
from English into Spanish.

00:15:07.387 --> 00:15:10.431
Remember, this is
50 million dollars worth of value.

00:15:10.431 --> 00:15:12.898
If we wanted to
translate Wikipedia into Spanish

00:15:12.898 --> 00:15:15.613
we can do it in five weeks
with 100 thousand active users,

00:15:15.613 --> 00:15:18.383
and we can do it in about 80 hours
with 1 million active users.

00:15:18.383 --> 00:15:20.752
Since all of the projects
my group has worked on so far

00:15:20.752 --> 00:15:22.179
have gotten millions of users,

00:15:22.179 --> 00:15:23.974
we're hopeful that we would be able

00:15:23.974 --> 00:15:26.223
to translate extremely fast
with this project.

00:15:26.223 --> 00:15:29.153
Now, the thing that
I am most excited about Duolingo

00:15:29.153 --> 00:15:31.594
is that I think this provides
a fair business model

00:15:31.594 --> 00:15:32.716
for language education.

00:15:32.716 --> 00:15:33.838
So here's the thing:

00:15:33.838 --> 00:15:36.322
the current business model
for language education is:

00:15:36.322 --> 00:15:37.815
the student pays.

00:15:37.815 --> 00:15:41.784
And, particularly, the student
pays Rosetta Stone 500 dollars.

00:15:41.784 --> 00:15:44.209
That's the current business model.

00:15:44.209 --> 00:15:46.039
The problem with this business model

00:15:46.039 --> 00:15:48.218
is that 95% of the world's population

00:15:48.218 --> 00:15:49.443
doesn't have 500 dollars.

00:15:49.443 --> 00:15:52.462
So it's extremely unfair towards the poor,

00:15:52.462 --> 00:15:54.503
this is totally biased towards the rich.

00:15:54.503 --> 00:15:58.145
In Duolingo, because while you learn

00:15:58.145 --> 00:16:00.261
you're actually creating value,

00:16:00.261 --> 00:16:02.498
you are translating stuff
which, for example,

00:16:02.498 --> 00:16:04.247
we could charge somebody for translation

00:16:04.247 --> 00:16:06.244
-- this is how we could monetize this.

00:16:06.244 --> 00:16:08.677
Since people are
creating value while learning,

00:16:08.677 --> 00:16:11.602
they don't have to pay with their money,
they pay with their time.

00:16:11.602 --> 00:16:14.545
But the magical thing here is that
they're paying with their time

00:16:14.545 --> 00:16:16.789
but that is time that would
have to be spent anyway,

00:16:16.789 --> 00:16:18.187
learning the language.

00:16:18.187 --> 00:16:19.956
The nice thing about Duolingo is

00:16:19.956 --> 00:16:22.491
I think it provides a fair business model,

00:16:22.491 --> 00:16:24.115
one that doesn't discriminate
against the poor people.

00:16:24.115 --> 00:16:26.279
So, here is the site:
[www.duolingo.com]

00:16:26.287 --> 00:16:27.521
(Applause)

00:16:27.521 --> 00:16:28.556
Thank you.

00:16:28.556 --> 00:16:32.458
(Applause)

00:16:35.298 --> 00:16:37.305
Here's the site.
We haven't yet launched

00:16:37.305 --> 00:16:40.679
but if you go there you can sign up
to be part of our private beta

00:16:40.679 --> 00:16:44.091
which we're probably going to start
in about three or four weeks.

00:16:44.641 --> 00:16:46.628
By the way, I'm the one talking here,

00:16:46.628 --> 00:16:49.036
but actually Duolingo is the work
of a really awesome team

00:16:49.036 --> 00:16:50.532
some of whom are here.

00:16:50.532 --> 00:16:52.186
So, thank you.

00:16:52.186 --> 00:16:54.881
(Applause)

